doc_read — implementation plan
==============================

status: done (2026-07-14)
"documentation for the agent (manuals, requirements, recap)".
wave 5 (documentation), pure scala — no MCP.ir, no ML. depends on
doc_list's Doc_Catalog (plans/doc_list Q1); implement doc_list
first.

the tool reads documentation from its PLAIN-TEXT SOURCES, never the
pdf: manuals resolve through the catalog to their src/Doc session's
chapter .thy files; NEWS/examples are plain files. the response
discipline is the lazy design used everywhere: no section argument
returns the table of contents (the map, small), a section argument
returns that section's text (the territory, capped).

advertised metadata (the builtin table row)
-------------------------------------------

name          doc_read
description   "Read Isabelle documentation from its plain-text
              sources. `name` is a doc_list entry (e.g. \"isar-ref\",
              \"system\", \"NEWS\"). For manuals: without `section`,
              returns the table of contents — chapter and section
              headings with their source file and line; with
              `section`, returns that section's source text
              (substring match on headings; an ambiguous match
              lists the candidates). Manual text is Isar theory
              source — prose with antiquotations — not the rendered
              pdf. For plain-text entries (NEWS, examples), returns
              file content; `lines` (e.g. \"120-180\") windows it.
              Long sections are truncated with a note; narrow with
              a more specific `section` or use search_sources over
              the manual's source session."
inputSchema   {"type": "object",
               "properties": {
                 "name": {"type": "string"},
                 "section": {"type": "string"},
                 "lines": {"type": "string"}},
               "required": ["name"]}
              (section and lines are mutually exclusive — section
              addresses manuals, lines addresses plain entries;
              handler-enforced, like find_definition's repl/theory.)
annotations   {"readOnlyHint": true, "idempotentHint": true,
               "openWorldHint": false}
outputSchema  none.

backing mechanism
-----------------

resolve `name` through Doc_Catalog (plans/doc_list). three entry
kinds, three behaviors:

manual (source session resolved):
  files: the session's theory sources in canonical order from the
  startup deps (Sessions.Deps -> session_theories + their paths —
  the same path map isabelle://theory/{name} resolution uses; one
  file per chapter in the doc sessions).
  toc: scan each file for heading commands (chapter | section |
  subsection | subsubsection) with their titles; reply rows
  {level, title, file, line}. computed lazily per manual, memoized
  (the sources are read-only distribution files).
  section read: match `section` against heading titles,
  case-insensitive substring. unique match -> the text from that
  heading to the next heading of the same or higher level (or end
  of file), truncation-capped (first window + "truncated, N more
  lines; narrow the section" note — the oversized-read rule from
  the resources design). multiple matches -> the matching toc rows
  ("it is a search, not a compile" — never guess). no match ->
  ok with "no section matching ..." + a hint to call without
  section (probe-safe).

plain entry (non-pdf path: NEWS, COPYRIGHT, examples):
  file content; `lines` windows exactly like isabelle://theory's
  ?lines= (reuse the parsing + truncation rules, not new ones).
  `section` on a plain entry -> argument error (there are no
  headings; the exclusivity story stays simple).

pdf only (no source session, pdf path — possible for user-extended
catalog entries; every bundled manual has sources):
  honest reply: "pdf only at <path>; no plain-text source in this
  distribution — the host agent can read the pdf directly." ok
  status, probe-safe.

unknown name -> ok with "no documentation entry ..." + nearest
doc_list hint (probe-safe, matching the discovery tools).

details to settle first
-----------------------

D1  heading extraction mechanism. two candidates:
    (a) line-anchored scan: a heading command at the start of a
        line (^chapter/^section/... followed by whitespace or an
        opening delimiter) with the title read from the following
        cartouche ‹...› or quoted string. the doc sources are
        hand-formatted prose theories; headings there are
        line-initial in practice.
    (b) token-based: outer-syntax token stream (isabelle.Token /
        Thy_Header + the session base's overall_syntax from deps),
        taking command tokens named chapter/section/... — immune to
        formatting, and the same machinery the
        plans/session_structure filesystem-/commands spike needs.
    decision rule: if that spike has landed its scala-side
    outer-syntax spans by the time this is implemented, REUSE it
    (b); otherwise ship (a) with the line-initial caveat pinned in
    a test (T6 guards against silent breakage across distribution
    upgrades). do not build (b) from scratch just for this tool.
D2  heading commands with markers/attributes (chapter \<open>..\<close>,
    section \<^marker>...): the doc sources use plain forms today;
    (a) must at least skip an optional target/marker before the
    title or tolerate extraction failure per-heading (drop the row,
    never crash the toc).
D3  cap sizes (revised 2026-07-14 during implementation): there was
    nothing to reuse — no ?lines= parser and no window constant
    exist anywhere in the codebase yet (isabelle://theory/{name}
    reads the whole file via start=0/stop=-1). doc_read is the
    FIRST tool to implement the spec's oversized-read rule, so it
    establishes Doc_Catalog.window_lines = 200 as the cap, applied
    to manual section reads and parameterless plain-entry reads
    alike; a chapter-sized section is EXPECTED to truncate — the
    note says how to narrow (a more specific section, or an
    explicit lines= for plain entries).

name/argument mapping across the layers
---------------------------------------

  mcp client edge   tools/call "doc_read",
                    {"name": "isar-ref", "section": "induction"}
  scala builtin row handler: exclusivity check (section|lines),
                    Doc_Catalog resolution, kind dispatch as above.
                    NO backend.ir call — file reads only, through
                    the deps path map. same shape as the
                    filesystem-tier theory resource read.

implementation steps
--------------------

1. heading scan (D1a or D1b per the decision rule) + toc as a pure
