assumptions and how to test them
--------------------------------

(scala-unit in mcp_test/src/mcp_handler_tests.scala — the real
distribution sources are available in-environment; bridge in
mcp_test/src/mcp_bridge_tests.scala. see plans/README "testing
process".)

T1  toc claim: doc_read {"name": "isar-ref"} returns headings from
    ALL chapter files of Isar_Ref (assert > 40 rows, at least one
    row per .thy file, every row carrying file + line, levels
    include chapter AND section).
    test [scala unit]: real sources.
T2  section extraction: a pinned section of a bundled manual (pick
    one at implementation time and hard-pin title + a contained
    phrase) returns text containing the phrase and NOT containing
    the following same-level heading's title.
    test [scala unit].
T3  search-not-compile: an ambiguous `section` substring returns
    the candidate rows, not text; an unknown section and an unknown
    `name` are ok-status "no ..." replies, not errors.
    test [scala unit]: all three.
T4  plain entries: doc_read {"name": "NEWS", "lines": "1-40"}
    returns exactly that window; `section` on NEWS is an argument
    error; section+lines together is an argument error.
    test [scala unit]: all three.
T5  truncation (D3): a chapter-level section read truncates at the
    window with the "narrow" note.
    test [scala unit].
T6  the D1a line-initial assumption (only if (a) ships): every
    heading command occurrence in the bundled Isar_Ref sources is
    found by the scanner — count(scanner) equals a raw token-level
    or grep-established count pinned at implementation time. this
    is the canary for distribution upgrades reformatting the docs.
    test [scala unit].
T7  full chain: the lookup workflow over a live server —
    doc_list -> doc_read {"name": "isar-ref"} (toc) ->
    doc_read {name, section} (text).
    test [bridge]: one case, three calls.

